pub const LESSON_GOALS : [27]str = [
    "Why a clean compile proves so little, and what a seal without output is worth.",
    "What a reference model is, and how a conformance suite holds a tool to it.",
    "How a verification plan is a checklist that a command can sweep and sign.",
    "The parts every *_tb.t27 spec has: stimulus, expected values, checks, a verdict.",
    "How to write a testbench that fails loudly, and why eyeballing waves is not a check.",
    "When one carefully chosen case beats a thousand random ones, and when it does not.",
    "What a .vcd file actually contains, and how to read its dump of every signal.",
    "How to go from a symptom to a signal, cycle by cycle, on a real measured bug.",
    "How to diff two runs and have the trace name the earliest cycle where they split.",
    "What a test vector is, and why the answer belongs beside the input, in the file.",
    "How a catalog works as a ruler: every population recounted, every count agreeing.",
    "How a scenario walks a whole flow, and what an end-to-end run proves that units cannot.",
    "How spec, simulator and board are held to one answer, and what each one is trusted for.",
    "What a simulator keeps (state) and what it does not (time between events).",
    "How to triage a disagreement: which of the three is lying, and how to find out.",
    "How line and toggle coverage count what a run touched, signal bit by signal bit.",
    "How state and transition coverage see the arcs a run never took.",
    "Why a coverage number can be a check that checked nothing, and how to spot it.",
    "What an assertion is in code, and what it costs to check one every cycle.",
    "How a bounded search explores every input up to k cycles, and what that proves.",
    "Why bounded is not a proof, and how induction closes the gap -- or reports it cannot.",
    "How mutation testing plants a bug on purpose and asks the tests to notice.",
    "What a surviving mutant tells you about the test that let it live.",
    "How to measure a suite, not admire it: caught mutants, deleted tests, vacuous passes.",
    "How one command re-runs every sweep and turns a build into a verdict.",
    "How the bench checks itself: every script running its own self-test, nothing skipped.",
    "Carry one module from spec to board with the whole verification stack behind it."
];
pub const LESSON_TEXTS : [27]str = [
    "Everything here compiles. The UART testbench in the player is green on all seven backends, and it still proves almost nothing: a compile says the words parse and the types line up, not that the words are the ones you meant. The widget shows the bigger trap. Every merged spec in t27 carries a seal, a hash of what building it produced; 31 of 1,428 seals record no output at all, spread over 16 specs, and 3 of those are missing from the debt ledger that is supposed to name them. A seal with no output behind it is a green check with nothing under it -- verification begins exactly there.",
    "To judge an implementation you need an answer that did not come from it: a golden model. For a number format that is the paper's table; for a tool it is the reference it claims to match. The widget is the conformance sweep that holds t27's own FPGA toolchain to its reference: 17 of 17 conformance specs compile, their test blocks pass, and no generated file has drifted from what was committed. The lesson's spec is the frame all those testbenches share -- the shapes a tb must have before it can check anything.",
    "A test plan written after the code is a description of what the code does; written before, it is a claim the code must live up to. In this repo the plan is a spec, and a command sweeps it. The widget is that sweep for the bench tools: 14 of 14 pass their own self-tests in one run -- decode 18/18, repin 17/17 -- and any tool that cannot check itself is listed, not waved through. The lesson's spec is build_verify: the checklist of what a build must show before anyone calls it done.",
    "Every testbench in this repo is one .t27 file with the same skeleton, and the player compiles it like any other spec. Open fifo_tb: it builds the stimulus (bytes into a FIFO), the expected values (the same bytes, in order), the checks (one assert per invariant, so a failure names the one that broke), and a verdict (a count, not a feeling). The widget generates JavaScript from a spec in the browser and runs it -- the same generate-then-execute shape a testbench uses, small enough to watch happen.",
    "A testbench that prints waves and stops is a machine for producing staring. A self-checking one computes a verdict and exits nonzero when it fails, so a script can run a thousand of them and trust the exit code. The widget is the terminal for seven t27 backends: every command run on hello_world, 21 pass, 0 fail -- every pass earned by an assert that executed, not by finishing. The lesson's spec, spi_tb, ends the same way: a count of checks, and a failure mode that cannot be mistaken for success.",
    "A directed test is one case you chose because you know it bites: the empty FIFO, the full one, the wrap. A random one is cheap volume. They are not rivals; they are a sequence. The widget is the knockout experiment turned into a tool: three performance bits were claimed, and one knockout bitstream per bit -- the same design with that bit tied off -- proves each one matters. That is directed testing's whole argument: one bit, one prediction, one bitstream, measured.",
    "A waveform file is text, and reading it as text is a skill. A .vcd declares its timescale, names its scopes and variables, then records only changes: a timestamp, an identifier, a value. The lesson's spec, vcd_trace, models exactly that -- headers, changes, durations -- with tests over earliest and latest change. The widget is a real trace wrapped up as a card: the testbench toggled clk 585 times and left 4 signals at X for the whole run, both facts counted from the file, not remembered from the screen.",
    "Here is a bug found with waves, end to end. A UART link on the bench requested 115,200 baud and the wire carried 115,385 -- a +0.16 % error from the clock divider, invisible in code, obvious in a capture. Requested 921,600, the wire carries 923,077, the same fractional error; 3,000,000 divides exactly and is exact on the wire. The measurement names the fix: choose divisors that land on integers. Waves turn a symptom (bytes lost) into a number (0.16 %) into an edit.",
    "Two runs of the same testbench, one before a change and one after: the fastest question is where they split. A trace diff answers at the resolution of one signal change: the earliest cycle where the golden run says 1 and the new run says 0. The widget diffs files this way, frame by frame, and refuses to say 'same' when a byte differs. The lesson's spec is the testbench that drives the VCD comparison tool -- expected against actual, with the mismatch printed, not eyeballed.",
    "A vector is an input with the correct output written next to it, in a file a tool can read. That last clause is where vectors usually die: 34 vector files with 512 cases live in the corpus, and the sweep behind the widget counted 2 files actually executed, 8 recorded as debt, 24 prose-only -- descriptions of cases no runner can parse. 19% carry data. A vector that nothing executes is documentation wearing a testbench's clothes.",
    "A ruler only works if it does not stretch. The formats catalog is t27's ruler for number formats, and the widget is the check on the ruler itself: four census populations -- counted in the catalog, recounted another way -- all agree, and the three that cannot be checked are named as uncheckable rather than rounded into agreement. That is the pattern to copy: recount every count a second way, and publish the ones you cannot.",
    "Unit tests prove the pieces; a scenario proves the pipe. The lesson's spec, e2e_demo, walks one design from spec to board in a single run, so a failure anywhere in the chain fails one place with a name. The widget draws the whole FPGA flow one layer per line -- spec, Verilog, cells, placement, bitstream, board -- and an end-to-end scenario is exactly that picture as a test: every layer either produces its artefact or stops the run.",
    "Three answers, one question. The spec says what should happen; the simulator says what a model of the silicon does; the board says what the silicon does. The widget is the rehearsal for that agreement with the board taken out of the loop: 26 checks, no board, no RTL -- a clean link returns every receipt with no retransmit, 11 loss and fault cases recover, and a lie, a duplicate or a wrong-key answer stops the run. When all three agree on the bench Artix-7 XC7A200T, you are done arguing.",
    "A simulator is state plus a queue of things that happen, and knowing that is what makes its output trustworthy. The lesson's spec, simulator, models the engine itself: states, probe points, trace entries, and the loop that drains events in order. The widget reads the real bench the way a simulator holds state: cables, boards, locks, and the last board runs -- three x7-board runs PASS 51840/51840 -- one command, one snapshot, no narratives between.",
    "When spec, simulator and board disagree, one of them is lying and the disagreement itself tells you where to look. If the simulator matches the spec but the board differs, suspect constraints, timing, or the model's assumptions -- pin assignments first, they are the usual liar. The widget is the triage tool for exactly that class: before an edit to a Verilog file, every spec and generator it would touch, so a fix cannot silently wander. Breadth first (what changes?), then depth (why does this one?).",
    "Line coverage asks: did anything execute this line? Toggle coverage asks a sharper question per bit: did this signal ever go 0-to-1, and ever 1-to-0? A stuck signal can sit under 100 % line coverage forever. The lesson's spec is coverage.t27 -- points with hit counts, per-kind percentages, and illegal arcs that must stay at zero -- with 26 tests and 4 invariants of its own. The widget bars a real design's resources; coverage bars are the same picture drawn over what a run touched.",
    "A state machine is verified by its arcs, not its states: 4 states with 12 transitions is 12 ways to be wrong, and visiting every state can leave half the arcs cold. The spec models it directly: state points and transition points, each with a hit count, and illegal transitions that fail the report the moment one warms. The widget is the discipline in another shape: 112 files pinned by sha256 in specs, every one accounted for -- 0 lost, 0 not kept, 0 unlocated. Nothing on the list is allowed to be unvisited.",
    "A coverage percentage is a claim about the tests, and claims can be empty. The widget found the empty kind: 11 specs refused at parse, 0 blocked only by prose, and 23 broken on purpose as fixtures -- but elsewhere in the corpus a 'test' that is only prose passes every check while executing nothing. The tells are always the same: a number with no denominator, a pass with no assert that ran, a sweep that cannot fail. Ask of every percentage: what would make this number move?",
    "An assertion is a check that stays in the design, every cycle, forever: not a testbench that runs once, but a sentence about the hardware that must hold while the hardware runs. The lesson's spec, formal, writes them as data -- immediate and concurrent, severities, clock and reset named -- because an assertion you cannot enumerate is an assertion you cannot discharge. The widget is the same idea at the door: each dependency the tool needs, present or not, checked in one call before anything runs.",
    "Bounded model checking is exhaustive search with a horizon: every input, every state, up to k cycles -- does any of them break the assertion? Inside the bound it is a proof, not a sample; no random seed can miss what the search enumerates. The lesson's spec carries the shape: a depth, a timeout, and properties to check. The widget is a bounded search in another domain: 84 board and model logs scanned for a key that must appear in none -- 0 hits, 0 logs too big to read -- absence, checked exhaustively rather than assumed.",
    "Bounded means bounded: k cycles checked is all k proves. To close the gap you need induction -- assume the invariant held up to cycle k, show it holds at k+1 -- and induction is honest in a way BMC is not: it can fail, and a failed induction is a finding, not an embarrassment. The widget is what an un-discharged obligation looks like when nobody looks: 12 Lean files, 15,553 lines and 4 sorry, reached by no build root -- nothing compiles them, so the proofs they contain have never been checked. An obligation no root reaches is not discharged; it is parked.",
    "Mutation testing inverts the whole game: instead of asking whether the tests pass, break the design on purpose and ask whether the tests notice. The widget is the tool doing it to a bitstream path -- plant a bug, watch the tests catch it -- and the lesson's spec is the command that owns the planting. A test suite that passes everything and catches no mutant is a suite that cannot fail, and a suite that cannot fail is not protecting anything.",
    "A surviving mutant is the most specific bug report you will ever get: here is a fault, here is the test that should have caught it, here is why it did not. The widget counted them where nobody was looking: 367 PRs landed on t27 in 14 days, and 11 of them deleted tests or asserts; one PR, titled to sound like an implementation, deleted all 26 tests in its file and nothing failed. Every one of those 11 was a mutant that survived -- and it survived because a human let it, which is exactly the failure mode mutation testing exists to catch.",
    "Coverage says what you ran; mutation says what your running is worth. A suite's strength is its kill count on faults you chose, its behavior when tests are deleted, its share of passes that executed an assert. The widget is the reference side of that measuring: all 6 reference bitstreams present and read -- 84.4 ms, best of 5 -- so a speed or strength number always stands on a reference somebody pinned. Measure the suite, or admit you are admiring it.",
    "A build is not a verdict until one command can re-derive it. The widget is that command for the bitstream stack: CRC 12/12 over the references, ECC 32520/32520 frames, 82/82 pins, 4224/4224 segbits, gold 5/5 -- every sweep re-run, every count with its denominator, and the last line says audit: CLEAN. The lesson's spec, build_verify, is the same list as a spec. If a person has to remember to run the checks, the checks will someday not be run.",
    "The bench checks itself before it checks your design: 16 spec-named scripts run their own --self-test, 0 bad, and the 8 scripts that have no such flag are listed, not silently skipped -- the difference between 'all good' and 'all we measured'. The widget is that sweep. The lesson's spec is fpga-selftest, the inventory of what a self-test is. A bench that cannot say which of its tools are untested is a bench whose verdicts carry unknown error bars.",
    "One module, the whole stack. Take a design through the player (spec clean), the testbench (checks executed, not skipped), the vectors (answers beside inputs), cosimulation (spec, simulator, board agreeing on the bench Artix-7 XC7A200T), coverage (lines, toggles, arcs, illegal arcs cold), formal (assertions bounded, induction attempted, honestly), mutation (kill count on planted faults), and sign-off (one command, every receipt). The widget is the flow running for real: place, route, a loaded bitstream, the clock on screen. Nothing in this course is a step you outgrow."
];
pub const LESSON_TASKS : [27]str = [
    "Open the seal sweep and find the 16 specs with no output; then open uart_tb in the player and say out loud what a compile of it does not prove.",
    "Open tri-fpga-specs and read one conformance spec's test blocks; then open testbench.t27 and name the four shapes a tb must have.",
    "Run the tool sweep and find the one tool whose self-test is listed rather than run; then read build_verify's checklist and mark which items are counts and which are claims.",
    "Generate JavaScript for the fifo_tb spec in the player and run it; find the assert that would fail first if the FIFO dropped a byte.",
    "Open the seven-backend terminal and find one command whose pass ran zero asserts; then check spi_tb for a failure mode that cannot print 'ok'.",
    "Pick one performance bit in the knockout widget and say what the no-knockout run would have to show for the bit to be a lie.",
    "Read the wrapped trace card: 585 toggles, 4 signals at X; then find in vcd_trace the function that counts changes at one timestamp.",
    "Compute what divider would put 115,200 on the wire exactly; check it against the 921,600 and 3,000,000 rows in the widget.",
    "Open the diff widget on two files of your own; then find in the compare testbench where the earliest mismatching cycle would be printed.",
    "Count the vector files in the widget's census that no runner executes; then write one vector, with its answer, in a file a tool could parse.",
    "Open the census widget and name the three populations marked uncheckable; then read the formats catalog and find one count stated twice.",
    "Walk the flow widget layer by layer and say what artefact each layer must produce; then find in e2e_demo where a missing artefact stops the run.",
    "Find the 11 recovery cases in the batch rehearsal and the three answers that stop the run; then say which of the three (spec, simulator, board) each one guards.",
    "In simulator.t27 find the loop that drains events and say what it would do if two events shared a timestamp; then compare with the status widget's one-snapshot read.",
    "Make the triage list: for each mismatch direction (spec vs simulator, simulator vs board), name the file you would open before any other, and why.",
    "Count the points in coverage.t27's fsm_report test and check the percentage by hand; then find one signal in the resource widget a toggle count would flag.",
    "List the arcs of a 4-state FIFO FSM and mark which a read-only workload leaves cold; then find the illegal arcs in the spec and what warms them.",
    "In the prose sweep, find the difference between 'refused at parse' and 'blocked only by prose'; then write a test whose pass executes no assert.",
    "Write one immediate and one concurrent assertion for a FIFO, with clock and reset named; then check the dependency widget for what tri itself assumes.",
    "Set a depth k and say exactly what your BMC run proves and does not; then find in the key sweep what '0 hits' took to be checkable rather than assumed.",
    "Find the 4 sorry in the Lean sweep and say what a build root that reached them would change; then name one invariant of uart_tb you would try to induct.",
    "Plant a bug with the mutate tool and watch which test catches it; then read the spec and find where the planted fault is named.",
    "Open the test-touch widget and find the PR that deleted all 26 tests; then say what check would have caught it at review time.",
    "Measure one thing twice, two ways, in the refs widget's style; then say which of your own suite's numbers has no denominator.",
    "Run the audit and find the one count with the largest denominator; then read build_verify and mark which checks are re-derived, not remembered.",
    "Open the self-test sweep and find the 8 scripts without a flag; then say what listing them, instead of skipping them, buys you.",
    "Choose a module of your own and write its verification plan: vectors, tb checks, coverage targets, assertions, mutants, sign-off; then run the flow widget end to end and mark where your plan would have stopped a real bug."
];

; --- Claims --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
test the_course_is_three_cubed {
    assert LESSONS_PER_MODULE == 3;
    assert MODULE_COUNT == 9;
    assert LESSON_COUNT == 27;
    assert MODULE_COUNT * LESSONS_PER_MODULE == LESSON_COUNT;
    assert LESSON_COUNT == 3 * 3 * 3;
}

test it_starts_with_a_bug_and_ends_with_a_verdict {
    assert MODULE_IDS[0] == "why-verify";
    assert MODULE_IDS[8] == "sign-off";
    assert LESSON_MODULES[0] == "why-verify";
    assert LESSON_MODULES[26] == "sign-off";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[26] == "perf2-loop";
}

test the_coverage_module_opens_the_coverage_spec {
    assert LESSON_MODULES[15] == "coverage";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[15] == "specs/fpga/coverage.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[16] == "specs/fpga/coverage.t27";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[24] == "tri-x7-audit";
}

test the_mutation_module_breaks_on_purpose {
    assert LESSON_MODULES[21] == "mutation";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[21] == "tri-x7-mutate";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[22] == "test-touch";
}

test the_course_sends_nothing {
    assert SENDS_NOTHING == true;
    assert LOCALES[0] == "en";
    assert LOCALES[1] == "ru";
}
